  "client_view_text": " Here\u2019s a statement that you can use as a starting point:\nA write-ahead log is a record of every change that has been made to the database, and it allows the database to recover after a crash by replaying the changes recorded in the log.\nA database crash can result in data loss or corruption, which can have serious consequences. A write-ahead log helps prevent this by keeping a record of every change made to the database before it is actually written to the database files. This log is essentially a journal of changes that have been made to the database.\nIf the database crashes, the write-ahead log can be used to recover the lost data. The database can simply replay the changes recorded in the log, effectively undoing the changes that were not written to the database files before the crash. This ensures that the database is returned to a consistent state, even in the event of a crash. The write-ahead log can also be used to implement database backups, allowing the database to be restored to a previous state.\n\nIn some database systems, the write-ahead log is also used to support concurrent transactions, allowing multiple users to update the database simultaneously without interfering with each other. The log helps to ensure that the database remains consistent by recording the changes made by each transaction, allowing the database"
